# Security Rules

Never commit secrets.

Never hard-code:

- API keys
- passwords
- OAuth tokens
- database credentials
- Twilio credentials
- OpenAI API keys
- HubSpot tokens

Use environment variables.

Maintain `.env.example` containing variable names but never real credentials.

Every tenant-specific operation must be authorized.

Every business-specific database query must be tenant-scoped.

Never trust tenant IDs supplied directly by the client.

Determine the current business/tenant from authenticated context.

Users must only access data belonging to businesses they are authorized to access.

External integrations must use secure credential storage.

Never expose server-side credentials to browser/client code.

Do not log secrets.

Do not log authentication tokens.

Do not include credentials in error messages.

Validate user-controlled input.

Validate tool inputs.

Validate external API responses.

Treat webhooks as untrusted input.

Verify webhook authenticity where the provider supports signature verification.

For sensitive operations, require appropriate authorization before execution.

Security must not be bypassed merely to make a test pass.

When security requirements are ambiguous, prefer the safer behaviour and flag the ambiguity.
